    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The khanate of Jochi's descendants, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu</a> after the Mongol conquest of the western steppe and Rus' in 1236–1242.</li><li>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> its khans took tribute from the Rus' princes and traded with Genoese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</li><li>Civil war after 1359 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s campaigns broke it into the khanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a> and the Great Horde.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name Golden Horde was coined by Russian writers centuries later; contemporaries called the state the Ulus of Jochi, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s eldest son.</p></aside>
    </section>

