    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="Genghis Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Golden Horde grew out of the share of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> assigned to his eldest son Jochi: the western steppe beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> and whatever lands further west the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> might conquer. Jochi died around 1225–1227, before his father, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu</a> inherited leadership of the western wing. In 1235 an assembly (<em>quriltai</em>) at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> ordered a great campaign to the west, and from 1236 Batu, with the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and princes of all branches of the ruling house, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchak</a> (Cuman) steppe and the principalities of Rus'. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> fell in December 1237, Vladimir in February 1238 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in December 1240. In 1241 Mongol armies defeated Polish and German forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Hungarians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohi">Mohi</a>, but in 1242, after news of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> Ögedei's death, Batu withdrew to the lower Volga. There he established his own ulus, the core of what later Russian sources called the Golden Horde, while his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orda-khan">Orda</a> led the eastern wing in what is now Kazakhstan. Batu built his capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, on the lower Volga, and Rus' princes travelled there, and sometimes on to Karakorum, to receive patents of authority. Contemporaries called the state the Ulus of Jochi; the name Golden Horde is attested only from the sixteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gurganj"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gurganj.jpg" alt="Gurganj" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gurganj">Gurganj</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urgench.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Atilin at French Wikipedia., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> were a small ruling minority in the Golden Horde. Most of the steppe population was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchak-languages">Kipchak Turkic</a>, and by the fourteenth century the Mongol elite had adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchak</a> language and merged into the Turkic population that the Russians called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>. Pastoral nomadism remained the basis of life on the steppe, with the khan's camp migrating seasonally along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, but the khanate also ruled settled regions: the towns and farmland of Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> with its city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurganj">Urgench</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, and the tribute-paying principalities of Rus'. Its wealth depended heavily on trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> grew into a large city with mosques, workshops and merchants' quarters, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who visited in the early 1330s, counted it among the finest cities he had seen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">Caravan routes</a> ran from Sarai to Khwarazm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, while the Genoese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venetians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tana-azov">Tana</a> on the Don, trading under the khans' licence, shipped grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, fish and slaves to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Enslaved Kipchak youths were sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Egypt</a>, whose rulers became allies of the khans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a> (r. 1257–1266) was the first khan to become Muslim, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbek-khan">Uzbek Khan</a> (r. 1313–1341) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> became the religion of the state, though the khans protected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> and exempted its clergy from tribute.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo.jpg" alt="Battle of Kulikovo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Battle of Kulikovo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dmitry_Donskoy_in_the_Battle_of_Kulikovo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adolphe Yvon, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Golden Horde inherited the military system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. Its army was organised in decimal units of tens, hundreds, thousands and ten-thousands (<em>tümen</em>), formed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> drawn from the nomadic tribes, increasingly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchak</a> rather than Mongol, and led by the khan's relatives and the heads of the leading clans. Its strength lay in mobility, discipline and the ability to raise large armies quickly from the steppe. The conquest of Rus' showed its skill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-warfare">siege warfare</a>, and for a century after 1242 punitive expeditions, such as the devastating campaign of 1293 led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duden">Duden</a>, kept the Russian principalities obedient. From 1262 the khans fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate of Persia</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, a war that drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a> into alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>. The general Nogai led raids into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Byzantine Thrace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and became a kingmaker until Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhta">Tokhta</a> defeated and killed him around 1299–1300. The army's weakness showed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-troubles">Great Troubles</a>: in 1380 the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> was defeated by Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry of Moscow</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Battle of Kulikovo</a>, although Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> two years later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> defeated Tokhtamysh at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-kondurcha-river">Kondurcha River</a> in 1391 and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terek-river">Terek</a> in 1395. Even so, the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a> crushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas of Lithuania</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-vorskla-river">Battle of the Vorskla River</a> in 1399 and besieged Moscow in 1408.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mengu-timur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mengu-timur.jpg" alt="Mengu-Timur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mengu-timur">Mengu-Timur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mengu-Timur_portrait._Timurid_genealogy,_1405-1409,_Samarkand_(Topkapi_Sarayi_M%C3%BCzesi,_H2152)_folio_36b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1405-1409 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Supreme power belonged to the khan, who had to be a descendant of Jochi, chosen and enthroned by an assembly of princes and leading nobles. Until the 1260s the khans acknowledged the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mengu-timur">Mengu-Timur</a> (r. 1266–1280) was the first to strike coins in his own name, marking the khanate's effective independence. A chief commander, the <em>beklerbeg</em>, and senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of the leading clans assisted the khan, and in the later Horde and its successor khanates the heads of four leading clans formed a council that could make or unmake khans. The khanate ruled its settled subjects indirectly. In Rus' it conducted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> in the 1250s, placed <em>darughachi</em> and <em>basqaq</em> officials to supervise tribute and recruitment, and granted princes patents (<em>yarlyk</em>) to rule. From the early fourteenth century the khans left the collection of tribute to the grand prince of Vladimir, an office that the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow">Ivan I Kalita</a>, came to monopolise by competing for the khans' favour. The khans also maintained a network of post stations (<em>yam</em>) and customs posts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbek-khan">Uzbek Khan</a>'s reign was the high point of central authority; but the system depended on a strong khan and an agreed succession, and after 1359 rival claimants and powerful emirs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a> dominated the state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="Moscow" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_and_the_Red_Square.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Department of State, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Golden Horde shaped the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the western steppe for centuries. Its long overlordship, known in Russian historiography as the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> yoke', separated the northern principalities from their western neighbours, drained them of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> through tribute and favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, whose princes rose as the khans' agents. Historians disagree on how far Muscovite institutions were borrowed from the Horde, but the postal system, customs and taxation practices and words such as <em>den'gi</em> (money) and <em>tamozhnya</em> (customs) show its influence. The Horde's role in trade was also far-reaching. By linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> it sustained the Italian colonies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, and the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> in 1346, during an epidemic in the besieging army, is often linked with the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through Genoese ships, though the exact routes of the plague remain debated. The alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Egypt</a> supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchak</a> recruits to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> army and kept a diplomatic channel open between the steppe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Its successor states, the khanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, Crimea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a> and Sibir, carried its political traditions into the early modern period, the Crimean Khanate surviving until 1783, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> all trace parts of their history to the Ulus of Jochi.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-troubles"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-troubles.jpg" alt="Great Troubles" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-troubles">Great Troubles</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tibicena, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of the Golden Horde began with a crisis of succession. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> struck the khanate's cities from 1346, disrupting trade and reducing the population. In 1357 Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Janibeg</a> was murdered by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berdibeg">Berdibeg</a>, who was himself killed in 1359; the line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu</a> died out, and two decades of civil war, the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-troubles">Great Troubles</a>', followed, in which more than twenty khans held the throne in quick succession while the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> ruled the west through puppet khans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jochids">Jochid</a> prince from the east backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, reunited the Horde after 1378, overthrew Mamai and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1382. He then turned against his patron, and Timur's campaigns of 1391 and 1395–1396 destroyed his armies and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tana-azov">Tana</a> and other trading cities, ruining the khanate's commercial base. The emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a> restored some power, but after his death in 1419 the Horde fragmented. The Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> emerged around 1438, the Crimean Khanate under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haci-i-giray">Hacı Giray</a> around 1441 and Astrakhan around 1466, while the remaining core became known as the Great Horde. Moscow under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> stopped paying tribute, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-stand-on-the-ugra-river">Great Stand on the Ugra River</a> in 1480 ended without battle when Khan Ahmed withdrew. In 1502 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menli-i-giray">Meñli I Giray</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> defeated Sheikh Ahmed, the last khan of the Great Horde, and sacked Sarai.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Golden Horde, the Mongol khanate of the western steppe, ruled the Rus' principalities for more than two centuries, grew rich on trade linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and shaped both the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> khanates that succeeded it.</em></p>

